{"name":"Fly a Kite","hex":"#c8daf5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fly a kite","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To raise money on commercial notes; to obtain money as by accommodation bills, the endorser having no actual money.","Synonym of go fly a kite."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fly_a_kite"}],"entries":[{"title":"Trial balloon","blocks":[{"text":"A trial balloon, or kite-flying, is information sent out to the media in order to observe the reaction of an audience. It is used by companies sending out press releases to judge customer reaction, and by politicians who deliberately leak information on a policy change. The term is of French origin. Trial balloon translates French ballon d'essai, which was a small balloon sent up immediately before a manned ascent to determine the direction and tendency of winds. Its earliest use in English is figurative."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/US_Navy_070628-N-7981E-056_Aerographer%27s_Mate_3rd_Class_Doron_Dill_and_Aerographer%27s_Mate_Airman_Shane_Green_launch_a_weather_balloon_off_the_fantail_of_Nimitz-class_aircraft_carrier_USS_Abraham_Lincoln_%28CVN_72%29.jpg/330px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trial_balloon"},{"title":"Fly","blocks":[{"text":"A fly is an insect of the order Diptera, named for the single pair of wings. The hindwings have evolved into halteres, organs that sense rotation, giving flies precise control of their flight. Diptera is a large order containing more than 150,000 species, including horse-flies, crane flies, hoverflies, mosquitoes and others."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Bessenbandzweefvlieg_Vrouwtje_%282%29.JPG/330px-Bessenbandzweefvlieg_Vrouwtje_%282%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fly"},{"title":"Kite","blocks":[{"text":"A kite is a tethered heavier-than-air craft with wing surfaces that react against the air to create lift and drag forces. A kite consists of wings, tethers and anchors. Kites often have a bridle and tail to guide the face of the kite so the wind can lift it. Some kite designs do not need a bridle; box kites can have a single attachment point. A kite may have fixed or moving anchors that can balance the kite. The name is derived from the kite, the hovering bird of prey. There are several kite shapes, including the popular delta kite and diamond kite."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Kitesflying.jpg/330px-Kitesflying.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kite"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Anthomyiidae_%28female%29_%2810144905255%29.jpg/500px-Anthomyiidae_%28female%29_%2810144905255%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Anthomyiidae_%28female%29_%2810144905255%29.jpg/1280px-Anthomyiidae_%28female%29_%2810144905255%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Anthomyiidae_%28female%29_%2810144905255%29.jpg/500px-Anthomyiidae_%28female%29_%2810144905255%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An Anthomyiid species showing characteristic dipteran features: large eyes, small antennae, sucking mouthparts, single pair of flying wings, hindwings reduced to clublike halteres","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnthomyiidae_(female)_(10144905255).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/RHYACOPHILA_DORSALIS_Male_Pont_Forge_de_Sailly_Watigny_02_MHNT.jpg/500px-RHYACOPHILA_DORSALIS_Male_Pont_Forge_de_Sailly_Watigny